// ScaleCorpus — the generated ladder the scale suite measures.
//
// ONE PROGRAM PER RUNG, and each rung DOUBLES all fifteen structural knobs at once. Six rungs is a
// 32x span in six compiles. The alternative — a matrix, one axis per knob — would be fifteen ladders
// and ninety compiles to learn the same thing, because the question is never "which knob is
// the compiler slow in" (the per-phase table answers that already) but "does the whole thing stay
// linear as a program grows", and a program grows in every direction at once. (Two SHIFT shapes ride
// along that are COVERAGE, not knobs — they are fixed-size and do not double; see `shiftCoverageSource`.)
//
// ⚠ THE CORPUS NOW DECLARES ITSELF. Every run prints `printCorpusManifest` — the constructs it
// generates, the dimensions it scales, and, load-bearing, the ones it structurally CANNOT see (it
// measures memory, never time; it compiles a subset of Maxon, not the whole language). The corpus was
// blind in four measured dimensions at once and every blind spot was silent; an instrument that cannot
// name what it cannot see keeps producing confident zeros.
//
// ARCHITECTURE.md claims compilation is linear in program size, and quotes measurements to back it —
// 1.96 -> ~1.0 for a 3,200-`if` function, 40x faster, a 19,200-block function that used to
// SIGSEGV. Every one of those numbers was measured on throwaway generated programs that were never
// committed. This file is the corpus that makes those claims falsifiable, and the two headline
// shapes are deliberately reproduced at the top rung: rung 5's `longFunction` is 3,200 `if`s, and
// its `deepBlocks` is ~19,200 blocks.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// THE TRAP, AND WHY EVERY VALUE HERE IS SEEDED FROM SOMETHING OPAQUE
// ===========================================================================================
//
// `foldConstOperands` is a real pass in this pipeline. A naively generated benchmark — the obvious
// one, a pile of arithmetic on literals — FOLDS AWAY TO NOTHING. The suite would then measure an
// empty compile at every rung, fit a perfect straight line through it, and report that the
// compiler is beautifully linear. It is the "check the instrument before the subject" failure, and
// it produces the most convincing possible wrong answer.
//
// So every value chain in this corpus starts from something the folder provably cannot see
// through, and there are exactly two such things in shv2:
//
//   1. A LOOP-CARRIED `var`. Its value at the top of the loop is a phi of its initializer and
//      whatever the body last wrote, so it is not a constant — and shv2 has no SCCP to prove
//      otherwise. `scaleSeed` is one, and it is where every rung's data originates.
//   2. A CALL RESULT. shv2 has no inliner and no interprocedural constant propagation, so a
//      function's return value is opaque at the call site no matter how trivial the callee. Every
//      generated function therefore takes its seed as a PARAMETER and derives everything from it.
//
// The runner does not take this on trust. `assertCorpusCompiled` (below) re-verifies it on every
// single ladder, and a corpus that folded flat is a BROKEN RUN — the one thing this instrument
// exits non-zero for, because it means nothing was measured at all.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// A RUNG DIRECTORY HOLDS EXACTLY THE GENERATED SOURCES, AND NOTHING ELSE
// ===========================================================================================
//
// This is not tidiness. It is the reproducibility the whole instrument rests on, and it was BROKEN.
//
// `build <dir>` LISTS that directory, and every entry it finds costs the compiler a `FilePath` and a
// `String` — measured, exactly, at +13 allocations and +459 bytes per extra file, at every rung. The
// runner used to write each rung's EXECUTABLE and its METRICS TSV into the rung's own source
// directory, and `.scale-tmp/` is never cleaned. So the first run on a fresh checkout compiled a
// ten-file directory and every run after it compiled a twelve-file one, and the two disagreed by 26
// allocations and 945 bytes per rung.
//
// The numbers were therefore "bit-for-bit reproducible" only if you never deleted your temp
// directory — which is to say they were not reproducible at all, and the committed goldens that
// gated on them would have failed on any machine that had never run the suite before. (Found by
// exactly the delta report this instrument now prints, on a run that changed no compiler code.)
//
// The outputs now live in the corpus ROOT, outside every rung (see ScaleTestRunner), and
// `pruneRungDir` guarantees the invariant holds even for a `.scale-tmp` left behind by an older
// build. ⚠ It DELETES: `<root>/rung<N>/` is owned by this generator, including under
// `--emit-corpus=<dir>`.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// NOT COMMITTED, ON PURPOSE
// ===========================================================================================
//
// The rungs are generated into `.scale-tmp/` at run time (gitignored, exactly as `.spec-tmp/` is).
// Committing them would add megabytes of generated Maxon to the tree for no benefit: generation is
// deterministic, so the corpus is a pure function of this file, and this file IS the corpus.
// `--emit-corpus=<dir>` dumps a ladder anywhere you want to look at it.
//
// THE SUBSET: shv2 implements `int` AND `float` arithmetic (including integer `/` and `mod`),
// functions (<=6 params), `if`, `while`, `break`, `return`, body-local `let`/`var`, TOP-LEVEL
// `let`/`var`, `type` declarations with field reads, bitwise + shifts (`and`/`shl`/`shr`), and ranged
// typealiases. No `print`, no arrays, no strings, no `for`, no `match`, no `as` casts. Everything
// below stays inside it — and `printCorpusManifest` states exactly this boundary on every run, so a
// construct outside it (and the codegen change to it that would be invisible) is NAMED rather than
// silently absent.
//
// ⚠ THE SUBSET AND THE CORPUS ARE TWO DIFFERENT SETS, AND THE GAP BETWEEN THEM IS WHERE BUGS LIVE.
// This is the file's most durable defect, and it has now recurred FOUR times:
//   • `float` was in the subset for a full rung before one line of this file generated any. See
//     `FloatOpsBase` for what that cost (a panicking emitter, shipped).
//   • integer `/` and `mod` were in it since P1.0d.3 — the entire `idiv` fixed-register path — and
//     every `/` the corpus emitted was a FLOAT `/`. See `IntDivideBase`.
//   • top-level `let` and `var` were in it for TWO rungs, 36 spec tests' worth. See `GlobalConstsBase`.
//   • `type` declarations were in it from P1.0r, the rung that gave shv2 a HEAP. See `StructTypesBase`.
//
// The manifest named the first two as blind spots the whole time and they were read as footnotes. It
// did not name the last two AT ALL — see `printCorpusManifest`, which is now the load-bearing half of
// this file rather than its documentation.

// Which rung of the ladder, and how many of them. A rung DOUBLES the one below it, so these are
// exponents, not sizes: `--rungs=20` would not be a big run, it would be a program a hundred
// thousand times rung 0 that never finishes. Hence a hard, refused-not-clamped upper bound.
export typealias RungIndex = int(0 to 15)
export typealias RungCount = int(1 to 16)

export let MinRungCount = 1 as RungCount
export let MaxRungCount = 8 as RungCount
export let DefaultRungCount = 6 as RungCount

// Total bytes of generated source in one rung — the denominator of the degeneracy check.
export typealias SourceByteCount = int(0 to u32.max)

// The outcome of parsing `--rungs=<n>`. A union rather than a throw, because the caller is inside
// the argument loop and must neither diverge nor invent a value: `invalid` is recorded as a bad
// option and the driver then refuses to run at all.
export union RungCountArg
	valid(count RungCount)
	invalid
end 'RungCountArg'

// Parse `--rungs=<n>`.
//
// A rung count outside the ladder's range is REFUSED, never clamped. Each rung DOUBLES the program,
// so `--rungs=20` is not "a thorough run", it is a program a hundred thousand times rung 0 that will
// never finish — and a caller who asked for it and silently got 8 would believe they had measured
// something they had not.
//
// The digits themselves are `parseCount`'s job (ScaleTestRunner) — this only decides what a valid
// RANGE is. Two hand-rolled decimal loops in one suite would be one loop too many.
export function parseRungCount(raw String) returns RungCountArg
	let value = try parseCount(raw) otherwise 'notANumber'
		return RungCountArg.invalid
	end 'notANumber'

	if value < MinRungCount or value > MaxRungCount 'outOfRange'
		return RungCountArg.invalid
	end 'outOfRange'
	return RungCountArg.valid(value as RungCount)
end 'parseRungCount'

// The number of `if`s, files, functions... in one knob at one rung. Bounded well above the top
// rung's largest knob (rung 7's deepBlocks is 200 << 7 = 25,600).
export typealias KnobSize = int(1 to 1048576)

// ===========================================================================================
// The fifteen knobs, and their rung-0 sizes
// ===========================================================================================
//
// Each is `base << rung`. The bases are tuned so that rung 0 clears fixed process + stdlib
// overhead (a rung that compiles in 3 ms measures the OS, not the compiler) while the top rung stays
// tractable. They are the ONE place to retune if the ladder gets too slow or too fast.

// The realistic codebase shape: many small functions. Each is a few ops — a couple of arithmetic
// ops and a branch — which is what most real functions are.
let FunctionsBase = 16 as KnobSize

// Real source FILES, not one big one: this is what exercises `load`, `merge`, and the query spine,
// none of which a single-file corpus would touch at all. The `manyFunctions` above are distributed
// across them, so functions-per-file stays constant as both double.
let FilesBase = 4 as KnobSize

// ONE function, N sequential `if`s. The 3,200-`if` benchmark ARCHITECTURE.md quotes: at rung 5
// this is exactly 100 << 5 = 3,200.
let LongIfsBase = 100 as KnobSize

// ONE function, a huge BLOCK count. Each `if`/`else` contributes three blocks (then, else,
// continuation), so this is ~3x its knob: at rung 5, 200 << 5 = 6,400 if/elses = ~19,200 blocks —
// the shape that used to SIGSEGV, and the reason "no CFG traversal may recurse" is a rule. It is
// separate from `longFunction` because that one grows OPS in a mostly-straight line and this one
// grows the CFG.
let DeepBlocksBase = 200 as KnobSize

// N chained calls, each caller holding values live ACROSS its call — so this is the cross-call
// spill bracket knob, not merely a call-depth knob.
let CallChainBase = 8 as KnobSize

// ONE function, N pressured loops. Its base is the smallest of the six because its cost used to
// overtake everything else as the ladder climbed — TWICE, from two different sources, both now fixed.
//
// THIS KNOB IS LINEAR NOW, and the history is worth keeping because the comment here was WRONG in two
// successive ways, each naming a real bug that was then fixed elsewhere:
//
//   1. It read "KNOWN SUPERLINEAR: the splitter's driver recomputes liveness from scratch after every
//      split… regalloc:liveness grows x3.69 per doubling." True until `49b160ce2` made that driver
//      INCREMENTAL. `regalloc:liveness` reads x1.99 now.
//   2. Then the real bend was the PARSER: `parseWhileStatement` minted one header phi per mutable var
//      IN SCOPE, so a function whose `var`s accumulate paid Σk ≈ L²/2 phis — minted, carried through
//      four passes inflating every dense column sized by the block-arg id space, and then deleted by
//      `pruneDeadBlockArgs`. Fixed by `11b9bbcba`: a loop now carries a phi only for the vars its
//      extent ASSIGNS. `pruneDeadBlockArgs` reads x1.99, `elimTrivialBlockArgs` x1.97, and
//      `phase:parse` x1.99 — the tail is gone at the source, not subtracted from the reading.
//
// THE KNOB IS RIGHT TO STAY AS IT IS. A function with many loops and many mutable vars in scope is
// ordinary code, and this knob held the front end honest until the front end earned it: it is what
// made the O(V×B) SSA-construction cost visible, and it is what will catch the next thing that inflates
// the block-arg id space. What it must NOT carry is a claim about which pass is slow — twice that claim
// outlived the bug it named. If this knob bends again, MEASURE which phase, do not read this comment.
let PressuredLoopsBase = 2 as KnobSize

// ONE function, ONE chain of loops NESTED to depth N — distinct from `pressuredLoops`, which grows
// loop COUNT at depth 1. Depth is what the loop-phi token pre-scan climbs: `parseWhileStatement`
// re-derives each loop's extent from the tokens (`loopBodyEndIndex`) and reads its assigned names off
// them (`namesAssignedIn`), and a loop NESTED inside another has its tokens re-scanned once per
// enclosing loop. So D nested loops make the scan O(depth x tokens) — quadratic in TIME.
//
// ⚠ IT WILL LIKELY READ ~LINEAR HERE ANYWAY, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE BLIND-SPOT DECLARATION. The
// scan allocates ~one small `AssignedNameSet` per loop and mints one phi per carried var per loop —
// both LINEAR in depth — so its cost is in TIME, which scale-test does not measure. The knob exists so
// the shape is present the day the scan starts ALLOCATING per token; until then the manifest names it
// as the canonical example of a superlinearity this instrument cannot see. See `printCorpusManifest`.
//
// Its base is the smallest of any knob: the loops all carry the SINGLE accumulator `acc`, so peak
// register pressure is one value at ANY depth (an E5001-safe deep nest — a per-level counter would be
// live across every enclosing loop and trip E5001 at depth ~14). Base 2 reaches depth 64 at rung 5 —
// realistic in shape (real code nests loops to depth 3), super-realistic in magnitude, exactly as
// `longFunction`'s 3,200 ifs are.
let LoopNestBase = 2 as KnobSize

// ONE function, N float ARITHMETIC/COMPARE groups — the float twin of `longFunction`.
//
// ⚠ THE CORPUS GENERATED NO FLOAT AT ALL UNTIL 2026-07-15, AND THAT IS THE FIFTH CONSECUTIVE RUNG
// THIS LADDER WAS BLIND TO WHAT LANDED. P1.0d.4 built an entire SSE backend — a second register
// FILE, a second pool, a conversion band, a NaN-correct compare lowering — and every float number
// this instrument printed was byte-identical BY CONSTRUCTION, because nothing in the ladder emitted
// one float instruction. The manifest even SAID so, in plain text, under NOT GENERATED. It was read
// five times as a footnote about the reader's own measurement instead of a defect in the tool, and
// the corpus finds only bugs someone already thought to generate.
//
// WHAT IT COST: `float-phi-*` in specs-shv2/float-type.md. A float `var` mutated in a branch or a
// loop — `var f = 0.0` — PANICKED the x64 emitter, because `Parser.mintPhi` hardcoded every phi's
// `argType` to `i64` and P1.0d.4 turned that field into the phi's REGISTER FILE. The commonest float
// idiom there is, and both the suite and this ladder were blind to it. This knob generates float
// phis by construction, so that class of bug cannot hide here again.
let FloatOpsBase = 64 as KnobSize

// ONE function, N loops, each holding `FloatsLivePerSpillLoop` floats live ACROSS a call — the most
// expensive thing a float does on this target, and a shape no GPR knob can stand in for.
//
// EVERY XMM REGISTER IS CALLER-SAVED in shv2's Wave-1 pool: `callerSavedMask` takes all of
// `classRegisterMask(RegClass.xmm)`, and taking xmm6-15 as callee-saved is Wave 2 (Win64 preserves
// all 128 bits of them while `movsd` zeroes 64-127, so it needs 16-byte slots and leaf frame
// alignment together). So a float live across a call has an allowed set of EXACTLY ∅ and MUST go to
// memory — where `callChain`'s GPRs in the same position still have five callee-saved registers to
// hide in. This is the float FORCE-SPILL path, and it is forced spilling rather than E5001: the
// amended Rule 2 permits a spill whose placement is forced by a call bracket.
let FloatSpillLoopsBase = 2 as KnobSize

// The bounded set of float accumulators `scaleFloats` mutates — four, against an XMM file of
// `RegisterFileSize - FirstXmmRegisterNumber`. Small on purpose: the straight-line
// arithmetic must never spill, so this knob measures float ARITHMETIC and cannot quietly become a
// second spill knob. That shape is `scaleFloatSpill`'s job, and holding the two apart is the same
// separation `WorkingLocalsPerFunction` keeps against `pressuredLoops`.
let WorkingFloatsPerFunction = 4 as KnobSize

// How many floats each spill loop holds live across its call. Every one of them spills (allowed =
// ∅), so this is exactly the number of forced store/reload brackets per loop — the float twin of
// `PressuredValuesPerLoop`, and it needs no headroom calculation because the XMM file's answer for a
// value live across a call is ∅ at any pool size.
let FloatsLivePerSpillLoop = 4 as KnobSize

// ONE file, N top-level `let` constants — the constant ARENA, the initializer DFS and its memo.
//
// ⚠ TWO WHOLE RUNGS WERE INVISIBLE HERE, AND THE MANIFEST DID NOT MERELY MISS THEM — IT REPORTED THEM
// AS COVERED. P1.0d.5a landed top-level `let` (the suite went 281 -> 295) and P1.0d.5b landed
// top-level `var` (295 -> 317). This corpus generated NEITHER, at any rung. The float case at least
// printed "no float" in plain text under NOT GENERATED for five rungs, and was read as a footnote
// about the reader's own measurement. This one was worse: the manifest listed `let / var` under
// CONSTRUCTS GENERATED — meaning the BODY-LOCAL kind, the only kind the corpus had — and named the
// top-level kind under NEITHER heading. A gap nobody is told about reads as coverage; a gap filed
// under the wrong heading reads as coverage and cannot be caught by reading the manifest at all.
//
// ⚠ BASE >= 2. The two seed constants are literals and every other derives from the two below it (see
// `globalConstsSource`), so a base below two would emit more constants than the knob claims to.
let GlobalConstsBase = 32 as KnobSize

// Keeps the constant DAG's values bounded — see `globalConstsSource`. The DAG is Fibonacci-shaped, so
// without a modulus its values would leave i64 many times over by the top rung's thousandth term. Any
// modulus whose successors stay well inside i64 would do; a thousand keeps the emitted literals short.
let GlobalConstModulus = 1000

// ONE file, N top-level `var`s — `.data` slots, `GlobalDataTable.layOut`, and a
// `globalAddr`/`loadIndirect`/`storeIndirect` triple per access.
//
// ⚠ NOT `globalConsts` WITH A DIFFERENT KEYWORD. The two share a syntax and NOTHING else, which is
// exactly the trap that let both go unmeasured behind one manifest line. A `let` is folded into its
// uses and has NO BACKEND AT ALL; a `var` is storage that survives to the linker, lays out a section,
// and loads through an impure op. A change to the data-section layout moves this knob and cannot move
// that one. Two knobs that a future change must be free to move independently are not one knob — the
// rule `floatSpill` states against `pressuredLoops`.
let GlobalVarsBase = 32 as KnobSize

// The value each global `var` is seeded with, cycled so the `.data` image is not one repeated byte
// pattern. A top-level `var`'s initializer MUST be a constant expression (E2045 otherwise), so this is
// a literal and CANNOT be seeded from `scaleSeed()` — and it does not need to be. See
// `globalVarsSource` for why a global is fold-resistant for free.
let GlobalVarSeedModulus = 7

// ONE file, N `type` declarations — the PER-TYPE cost: the type table and the layout descriptor.
//
// ⚠ P1.0r GAVE SHV2 A HEAP AND THIS LADDER GENERATED NO `type` AT ALL. The tell was printed on every
// run and read six times as a flat line: `phase:runtimeAugment` went 54 -> 58 across the WHOLE
// ladder. That was never a runtime that scaled beautifully — it was a runtime that was never
// INSTALLED, because nothing in the corpus allocated.
//
// ⚠ AND THAT PHASE STILL WILL NOT MOVE WITH THIS KNOB — it is fixed-cost BY CONSTRUCTION, so do not
// read it as this knob's report card. `phase:runtimeAugment` times `augmentWithRuntime` only, which is
// `prependEntryStub`: ONE hand-built entry stub. The heap runtime proper (`installMmRuntime`, five
// fixed functions) is called at Compiler.maxon:180, OUTSIDE every `PhaseProbe`, so it is billed to no
// phase at all. What DOES prove the runtime is installed is the leak gate: a struct in a block exits
// 101, which is unreachable unless `__mm_leak_check` was linked in. This knob's real cost lands in
// `lowerMaxonToStd`, `isel`, `regalloc` and `encode`, where the work actually is.
let StructTypesBase = 8 as KnobSize

// ONE file, M `create` sites over a BOUNDED set of types — the PER-SITE cost: an `__mm_alloc` call, a
// field store per field, and the release the value's death forces.
//
// ⚠ NOT `structTypes` WITH A BIGGER NUMBER. One knob grows how many types the compiler must LAY OUT;
// this one grows how many times a type is BUILT. P1.1a wave 2 (field writes) moves this one and not
// that one; a change to the layout descriptor moves that one and not this one. Same rule as
// `globalVars` above, and as `floatSpill` against `pressuredLoops`.
let StructAllocsBase = 32 as KnobSize

// The bounded set of types `scaleStructAllocs` rotates over. CONSTANT on purpose: were the alloc knob
// to grow its own types it would BE `structTypes`, the two would move together for ever, and the
// separation defended above would exist only in the comments.
let AllocStructTypeCount = 4 as KnobSize

// ONE file, N `/` and `mod` groups — `StdOp.div`, and the x64 `idiv` path whose RAX/RDX pinning is the
// only HARD fixed-physical-register constraint in the language.
//
// ⚠ P1.0d.3 BUILT THAT WHOLE PATH — `cqo`, and the implicit-def masks that keep the divisor and every
// value live across the `idiv` out of RAX and RDX — and the corpus measured none of it. It was not
// even an obvious zero: the corpus was FULL of `/`, and every one of them was a FLOAT `/`, which is a
// different Std op entirely (`binOp(div)` — no fixed registers, no trap, a different register file).
// Counting the operator would have said "covered"; only counting its TYPE says otherwise.
let IntDivideBase = 32 as KnobSize

// Masks the opaque seed into a divisor that is provably in 1..8: `(acc and DivisorMask) + 1`.
//
// BOTH HALVES ARE LOAD-BEARING. A CONSTANT divisor folds to a shift and measures no `idiv` at all, so
// it must derive from the seed. And it must not reach ZERO: these programs are compiled and never run,
// so a `#DE` would never be observed here — which is precisely why it would be wrong to leave in. A
// corpus is a claim about the shape of real code, and real code does not divide by zero. Being
// non-negative also keeps it clear of `i64.min / -1`, the `idiv` path's other trap.
let DivisorMask = 7

// How many SCOPE-FILLER locals a long generated function carries — the V DIMENSION, and it DOUBLES.
//
// ⚠ THIS DOUBLES BECAUSE THE CORPUS WAS BLIND, AND IT WAS BLIND IN THE ONE DIMENSION IT NAMED ITSELF
// FOR. The parser builds SSA on the fly at a cost O(vars-in-scope) per branch — and until each was
// fixed, that cost was in BYTES: every `ParseExpression` snapshotted the variable registry (fixed with
// the ternary work), and every `if` snapshotted the whole mutable-var set (fixed by 8e10ce333). Both
// hid because `longFunction` and `deepBlocks` carried a FIXED handful of locals, so V never grew and no
// O(V·anything) cost could surface. **A fixed V is a V dimension the ladder cannot climb.** The comment
// that used to sit here defended the constant as "realistic, and that is the whole point" — and a review
// DISPROVED exactly that reasoning: real IS the shape, but the MAGNITUDE must be scaled past real, or
// the instrument sees nothing. `longFunction` already does this with 3,200 `if`s; this does it with locals.
//
// So V doubles, and the base is picked so the TOP rung reaches ~4,096 locals — into the thousands,
// which is where the deleted `ParseExpression` snapshot's O(V) cost actually bit (~4,000). Real
// functions top out near ~30 locals (measured across maxon-shv2 + stdlib: p99 = 16, max = 44), so
// this is deliberately super-realistic in magnitude and realistic in shape.
//
// WHAT THE LADDER NOW SEES (measured on current `main`, both V and the ifs doubling) — and this knob is
// now a REGRESSION GUARD on two fixes it first exposed:
//   • `phase:parse` ALLOCATIONS grow x2.00 — LINEAR. The `ParseExpression` HashSet snapshot that went
//     O(V) per call is GONE (deleted with the ternary short-circuit); its being linear confirms that.
//   • `phase:parse` BYTES grow ~x2.0 — LINEAR too, since 8e10ce333. This knob EXPOSED an O(V·ifs) BYTE
//     quadratic here (parse bytes read x3.9): each `if` snapshotted the whole mutable-var set — O(V) per
//     branch × O(ifs) = O(V·ifs) — invisible until V doubled. FIXED the way loops were (11b9bbcba): an
//     `if` now carries only the vars its token extent ASSIGNS, so the snapshot is O(assigned) and parse
//     bytes read x2.03. What is LEFT is an O(V)-per-`if` TIME scan of `mutableVars` (`assignedBindingsIn`)
//     that allocates nothing and is invisible to this memory instrument BY DESIGN — so if a future change
//     reintroduces an O(V) per-branch ALLOCATION, this column is what catches it.
let LocalsPerFunctionBase = 128 as KnobSize

// The BOUNDED set of accumulators the generated branches actually MUTATE — the "assigned" set, held
// apart from the "in-scope" set above so the two dimensions stay distinct.
//
// It is a small constant on purpose: values live across the branches must fit the 14-GPR pool, so the
// knob measures the PARSER (O(vars in scope)) and never accidentally becomes a second `pressuredLoops`
// by spilling. The V scope-fillers each fold into one of these the instant they are declared (see
// `fillerLocalsDecl`), so a filler's live range is length ~1 and the machine only ever holds these
// six — while all V+6 remain IN SCOPE for every branch's snapshot. That gap between "in scope" (V+6)
// and "live" (6) is the whole trick that lets V climb into the thousands without tripping E5001.
let WorkingLocalsPerFunction = 6 as KnobSize

// Declare the bounded working accumulators — the set the generated branches MUTATE — each seeded from
// the parameter `a` so the folder cannot see through them and fold the function away.
function workingLocalsDecl() returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	for w in 0 upto WorkingLocalsPerFunction 'decl'
		out.append("\tvar w{w} = a + {w + 1}\n")
	end 'decl'
	return out.build()
end 'workingLocalsDecl'

// Declare `localCount` SCOPE-FILLER locals — the V dimension. Each is folded into a working
// accumulator the instant it is declared, which is load-bearing in two independent ways:
//
//   IN SCOPE, NOT LIVE. Once declared, a filler stays in the parser's mutable-var set until the
//     function ends, so every later branch snapshots it — that is the O(V) parser cost this knob
//     exists to grow. But its VALUE is dead after the fold one line down, so the backend holds it for
//     a single instruction and never spills it. Declaring all V at the top and reading them at the END
//     instead (the old `sum`) makes all V LIVE at once and trips E5001 at V > ~13 — which is why that
//     shape could never have carried a doubling V at all.
//   NOT DEAD. The fold USES the filler, so no dead-code pass may drop it and quietly take the V
//     dimension away again.
function fillerLocalsDecl(localCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	for v in 0 upto localCount 'fill'
		out.append("\tvar l{v} = a + {v + 1}\n")
		out.append("\tw{v mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction} = w{v mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction} + l{v}\n")
	end 'fill'
	return out.build()
end 'fillerLocalsDecl'

// The full local prelude a scaling function opens with: the bounded working set the branches mutate,
// then `localCount` scope-fillers. Shared by `longFunction` and `deepBlocks` so the two measure the
// SAME V dimension against different CFG shapes rather than each spelling the prelude out.
function scopedLocalsDecl(localCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append(workingLocalsDecl())
	out.append(fillerLocalsDecl(localCount))
	return out.build()
end 'scopedLocalsDecl'

// The shared epilogue: sum the bounded working accumulators and RETURN the total. Only these six are
// read here, so peak pressure at the end stays at six — the fillers are long dead and cost nothing.
// It emits the `return` too, so `longFunction` and `deepBlocks` share the whole tail, not just the sum.
function workingLocalsSumAndReturn() returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\tvar sum = a\n")
	for w in 0 upto WorkingLocalsPerFunction 'sum'
		out.append("\tsum = sum + w{w}\n")
	end 'sum'
	out.append("\treturn sum\n")
	return out.build()
end 'workingLocalsSumAndReturn'

// A nesting-indent depth: bounded well above `LoopNestBase << MaxRungCount` plus the couple of levels
// the generator adds, so `d + 2` for the deepest loop stays in range.
typealias TabCount = int(0 to 2097152)

// `count` tab characters — the cosmetic indentation for the nested-loop knob. Maxon blocks are
// delimited by `end 'label'`, not by indentation, so this is purely for a human reading a dumped
// corpus; the parser's loop-phi scan counts `while`/`end` TOKENS and never looks at it.
function tabs(count TabCount) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	var n = count
	while n > 0 'each'
		out.append("\t")
		n = n - 1
	end 'each'
	return out.build()
end 'tabs'

// How many accumulators each pressured loop holds live ACROSS a call.
//
// EIGHT IS A CHOSEN NUMBER, not a round one. The allocatable pool is 14 GPRs, of which only 5 are
// callee-saved — so a value live across a call has just those 5 to live in, and the excess MUST go
// to memory. Eight accumulators plus the loop counter plus the running total is ten values live
// across the call: five forced spill brackets per loop, which is exactly the splitter work this
// knob exists to provoke.
//
// And it stays clear of E5001. Peak pressure here is eleven (the ten above plus the call's result),
// against a full pool of fourteen — so the loop FITS the machine, and the spills are the ABI's
// doing, not a register shortage. Raising this to fourteen-plus would make the corpus fail to
// compile with a register-pressure diagnostic and VOID the whole ladder. See
// `specs-shv2/register-pressure.md`, which spells out that a forced spill across a call is not
// E5001 and must never become it.
let PressuredValuesPerLoop = 8 as KnobSize

// Iterations of each generated loop. Small: the compiler never RUNS these programs, so trip count
// is irrelevant to the measurement — only the code shape is. Big enough not to look degenerate.
let GeneratedLoopTrips = 4

// ===========================================================================================
// Rung layout on disk
// ===========================================================================================

// Shared with ScalePerType, which locates the rungs this generator wrote, and with the runner, which
// names each rung's metrics + output file after it — in the corpus ROOT, never in the rung.
export let RungDirPrefix = "rung"
export let MaxonExtension = ".maxon"

// One generated rung: where it lives and how big it is. `sourceBytes` is the corpus self-check's
// denominator — the question "did the compiler's output grow with its input" needs both.
export type ScaleRung
	export let index as RungIndex
	export let dir as FilePath
	export let sourceBytes as SourceByteCount

	export static function create(index RungIndex, dir FilePath, sourceBytes SourceByteCount) returns ScaleRung
		return Self{index: index, dir: dir, sourceBytes: sourceBytes}
	end 'create'
end 'ScaleRung'

export typealias ScaleRungArray = Array with ScaleRung

// A knob's size at `rung`: `base << rung`. THE one place the doubling lives, so no knob can drift
// out of step with the others and quietly change what "one rung" means.
function knobAt(base KnobSize, rung RungIndex) returns KnobSize
	var size = base
	for _ in 0 upto rung 'double'
		size = size * 2
	end 'double'
	return size
end 'knobAt'

// Generate the whole ladder under `root`, returning one ScaleRung per rung.
export function generateCorpus(root FilePath, rungs RungCount) returns ScaleRungArray throws ScaleError
	if not Directory.create(root) 'noRoot'
		throw ScaleError.reason("could not create the corpus root {root.toString()}")
	end 'noRoot'

	var ladder = ScaleRungArray.create()
	for rung in 0 upto rungs 'eachRung'
		ladder.push(try generateRung(root, rung: rung as RungIndex))
	end 'eachRung'
	return ladder
end 'generateCorpus'

// Generate one rung: a DIRECTORY of `.maxon` files (shv2's `build <dir>` walks them recursively),
// so the `manyFiles` knob is expressed as real files on disk rather than simulated.
function generateRung(root FilePath, rung RungIndex) returns ScaleRung throws ScaleError
	let dirName = try FilePath.from("{RungDirPrefix}{rung}") otherwise panic("ScaleCorpus: rung dir name must be valid")
	let dir = root.join(dirName)
	if not Directory.create(dir) 'noDir'
		throw ScaleError.reason("could not create the rung directory {dir.toString()}")
	end 'noDir'

	let fileCount = knobAt(FilesBase, rung: rung)
	let funcCount = knobAt(FunctionsBase, rung: rung)
	let localCount = knobAt(LocalsPerFunctionBase, rung: rung)

	let chainLength = knobAt(CallChainBase, rung: rung)

	var sourceBytes = 0 as SourceByteCount
	var written = StringArray.create()
	// Every generated entry point `main` threads the accumulator through, IN CALL ORDER — filled by
	// the `writeRungFile` calls below as each file is written, read back by `main`. The generation
	// order below IS the call order, which is what lets one append-as-you-go list serve both.
	var drivers = StringArray.create()

	// `a_` / `b_` / ... prefixes only order the directory listing for a human reading a dumped
	// corpus; shv2 merges files in listing order and the merge is order-independent.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "a_seed", source: seedSource(), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threadsNothing)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeManyFunctions(dir, fileCount: fileCount, funcCount: funcCount, written: written, drivers: drivers)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "c_long", source: longFunctionSource(knobAt(LongIfsBase, rung: rung), localCount: localCount), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(LongIfDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "d_blocks", source: deepBlocksSource(knobAt(DeepBlocksBase, rung: rung), localCount: localCount), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(DeepBlocksDriver))
	// The chain's driver is its TOP link, whose name depends on the knob's size — so it is asked for
	// by the same function that generates the links, never respelled from the shape of the name.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "e_chain", source: callChainSource(chainLength), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(chainTopName(chainLength)))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "f_loops", source: pressuredLoopsSource(knobAt(PressuredLoopsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(PressuredDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "g_nested", source: nestedLoopsSource(knobAt(LoopNestBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(NestedDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "h_shifts", source: shiftCoverageSource(), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(ShiftsDriver))
	// The float knobs thread the seed exactly as the int ones do — each takes the opaque `acc` and
	// hands back a `trunc`ed int, so a float function is as load-bearing as any other and no DCE pass
	// could take the float dimension away again.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "i_floats", source: floatOpsSource(knobAt(FloatOpsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(FloatsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "j_fspill", source: floatSpillSource(knobAt(FloatSpillLoopsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(FloatSpillDriver))
	// `scaleGlobalConsts` is threaded exactly as the others are even though the folder eats its
	// constants: the call keeps the DECLARATIONS alive, which is the whole of what that knob measures
	// (see `globalConstsSource`).
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "l_consts", source: globalConstsSource(knobAt(GlobalConstsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(GlobalConstsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "m_globals", source: globalVarsSource(knobAt(GlobalVarsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(GlobalVarsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "n_stypes", source: structTypesSource(knobAt(StructTypesBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(StructTypesDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "o_sallocs", source: structAllocsSource(knobAt(StructAllocsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(StructAllocsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "p_divide", source: intDivideSource(knobAt(IntDivideBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(DivideDriver))
	// `z_`, not `k_`, so `main` sorts last however many knobs are added above it. The prefixes are
	// cosmetic (shv2 merges in listing order and the merge is order-independent), but a `main` wedged
	// mid-alphabet would have to be renamed by every future knob, and `pruneRungDir` deletes the old
	// `k_main.maxon` from a stale rung directory rather than leaving it to join the measurement.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "z_main", source: mainSource(drivers), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threadsNothing)

	try pruneRungDir(dir, generated: written)

	return ScaleRung.create(rung, dir: dir, sourceBytes: sourceBytes)
end 'generateRung'

// WHAT `main` CALLS IN ONE GENERATED FILE — the question every `writeRungFile` must answer, so that a
// knob CANNOT be added without deciding it.
//
// It is a REQUIRED parameter rather than a convention because the failure it prevents is silent. The
// call list used to be re-spelled by hand in `mainSource`, one line per knob; a knob whose author
// forgot the line still compiled, still generated its file, and still measured — right up until a DCE
// pass lands, at which point its now-uncalled function is legitimately deleted and that knob's
// dimension vanishes from the ladder while the numbers go on looking perfectly linear. There is no
// DCE today, which is exactly why this had to be fixed before there is.
union RungDriver
	// The file's single entry point, which `main` threads the accumulator through: `acc = name(acc)`.
	threads(name String)

	// The file contributes no single entry point of its own. Two shapes qualify, both real:
	// `writeManyFunctions`, whose N functions register themselves BY NAME as they are generated (one
	// file holds many, so the file has no one driver); and the two files `main` does not thread at all
	// — `a_seed`, whose `scaleSeed()` SEEDS the accumulator rather than taking it, and `z_main`, which
	// IS `main`.
	threadsNothing
end 'RungDriver'

// Write one generated file, returning its byte length so the caller can total the rung's source
// size without stat-ing the directory back, recording its NAME so the prune below knows it belongs
// there, and registering its entry point so `main` can call it without re-spelling the name.
function writeRungFile(dir FilePath, name String, source String, written StringArray, drivers StringArray, driver RungDriver) returns SourceByteCount throws ScaleError
	let fileName = "{name}{MaxonExtension}"
	let path = dir.join(fileName)
	try File.writeText(path, content: source) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not write {path.toString()}")
	written.push(fileName)

	match driver 'driver'
		threads(driverName) then drivers.push(driverName)
		threadsNothing then break 'driver'
	end 'driver'

	return source.byteLength() as SourceByteCount
end 'writeRungFile'

// Delete everything in the rung directory that this run did not just generate — see the header.
//
// A rung directory is not a place, it is a VALUE: it is exactly the program this rung's knobs
// describe. Anything else in it — a stale `b_funcs_9.maxon` from a run with a bigger `FilesBase`, an
// executable an older version of the runner wrote there — is compiled or listed by `build <dir>`, and
// so is part of the measurement, silently and unreproducibly.
//
// A stale SUBDIRECTORY is a THROW rather than a delete: `build <dir>` walks recursively, so its
// contents would be compiled, and the stdlib has no recursive remove with which to fix it. Refusing
// with the path is the honest answer; the fix is one `rm -rf` and it should be a person's decision,
// not this generator's.
function pruneRungDir(dir FilePath, generated StringArray) throws ScaleError
	let entries = try Directory.list(dir) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not list the rung directory {dir.toString()}")

	for entry in entries 'each'
		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'staleSubdir'
			throw ScaleError.reason("{entry.toString()} is a directory inside a rung, and `build <dir>` walks recursively — so it would be COMPILED as part of this rung and the measurement would be of a program this generator did not write. Delete the corpus root and re-run.")
		end 'staleSubdir'

		if generated.contains(entry.filename()) 'generated'
			continue
		end 'generated'

		try File.delete(entry) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not delete the stale file {entry.toString()} from the rung directory, and leaving it there would put it in the measurement")
	end 'each'
end 'pruneRungDir'

// ===========================================================================================
// The knobs, as source
// ===========================================================================================

// The seed, and the opaque callee — the two generated functions OTHER emitters name. `main` seeds the
// accumulator with `SeedName` (which is why `a_seed` is `threadsNothing`: it PRODUCES the accumulator
// rather than taking it), and the two spill knobs call `OpaqueCalleeName` across their live values.
// Named here, beside the generator that defines them, so a rename cannot leave a caller behind.
let SeedName = "scaleSeed"
let OpaqueCalleeName = "scaleOpaque"

// THE ORIGIN OF EVERY VALUE IN THE CORPUS, and the reason none of it folds away.
//
// `acc` is a loop-carried `var`: at the top of the loop it is a phi of its initializer and what the
// body last wrote it. `foldConstOperands` moves a CONSTANT operand into an immediate; it cannot
// prove a phi is a constant, and shv2 has no SCCP that could. So `scaleSeed()` is opaque, its
// return value is opaque, and everything downstream that derives from it is opaque too.
//
// This is the same trick `specs-shv2/register-pressure.md` uses to hold 17 values live without the
// folder eating them.
function seedSource() returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus. The opaque seed every other value derives from.\n")
	out.append("function {SeedName}() returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = 1\n")
	out.append("\tvar i = 0\n")
	out.append("\twhile i < {GeneratedLoopTrips} 'seedLoop'\n")
	out.append("\t\tacc = acc + i * 3\n")
	out.append("\t\ti = i + 1\n")
	out.append("\tend 'seedLoop'\n")
	out.append("\treturn acc\n")
	out.append("end '{SeedName}'\n")
	out.append("\n")
	// A callee with a branch in it, so a call to it is neither trivially foldable nor free. Every
	// pressured loop calls this ACROSS its live accumulators, which is what forces the spills.
	out.append("function {OpaqueCalleeName}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tif a > 100 'big'\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn a - 100\n")
	out.append("\tend 'big'\n")
	out.append("\treturn a + 1\n")
	out.append("end '{OpaqueCalleeName}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'seedSource'

// The `manyFunctions` + `manyFiles` knobs, which are one piece of work: N functions spread over M
// real files. Both double per rung, so functions-per-file is constant and the two knobs stay
// independent — M is what `load`/`merge`/the query spine see, N is what the pass pipeline sees.
//
// It appends every function's NAME to `drivers`, in the order it generated them. `main` calls them
// back from that list — so the enumeration (the ceiling division and the `>= funcCount` break) lives
// HERE, once. When it lived twice, `main`'s copy had to reproduce this one's names exactly or it would
// call an undefined `sfX_Y` (a broken run) or skip one (an uncalled function a future DCE deletes,
// silently flattening the knob — the very failure `RungDriver` now generalises to every knob).
//
// This is the ONE file whose functions register individually rather than through `writeRungFile`'s
// `driver` parameter: it writes N files holding N*k functions, so there is no single entry point to
// declare, and the names are known only inside the loop that mints them.
function writeManyFunctions(dir FilePath, fileCount KnobSize, funcCount KnobSize, written StringArray, drivers StringArray) returns SourceByteCount throws ScaleError
	// Ceiling division: the last file takes the remainder, and no function is dropped because the
	// counts did not divide evenly.
	let perFile = (funcCount + fileCount - 1) / fileCount

	var total = 0 as SourceByteCount
	for fileIndex in 0 upto fileCount 'eachFile'
		var out = StringBuilder.create()
		out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the many-small-functions knob, file {fileIndex}.\n")
		for slot in 0 upto perFile 'eachFunc'
			let funcIndex = fileIndex * perFile + slot
			if funcIndex >= funcCount 'past'
				break
			end 'past'
			drivers.push(smallFunctionName(fileIndex, slot: slot))
			out.append(smallFunctionSource(fileIndex, slot: slot, salt: funcIndex))
		end 'eachFunc'
		total = total + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "b_funcs_{fileIndex}", source: out.build(), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threadsNothing)
	end 'eachFile'
	return total
end 'writeManyFunctions'

// One small function of the realistic-codebase shape: a couple of arithmetic ops and a guarded
// branch. Everything derives from the parameter `a`, which is a call argument at every call site and
// therefore opaque to the folder.
//
// The guard is a SHORT-CIRCUIT `and`. This is the knob that doubles the FUNCTION COUNT, so it is
// where a per-function cost shows up — and short-circuit lowering is not free: it mints two blocks, a
// merge phi and a seed literal per `and`. The corpus contained no `and`/`or` at all until 2026-07-14,
// which meant the compare/branch fusion path — the commonest conditional shape in the language — was
// measured by nothing.
function smallFunctionSource(fileIndex KnobSize, slot KnobSize, salt KnobSize) returns String
	let name = smallFunctionName(fileIndex, slot: slot)
	// `salt` varies the constants across functions so they are not textually identical — identical
	// bodies would be a fair benchmark of nothing but the parser's cache.
	let k = salt mod 7 + 1
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\nfunction {name}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tlet b = a + {k}\n")
	out.append("\tlet c = b * 3\n")
	out.append("\tif c > a and b < c 'grew'\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn c - a\n")
	out.append("\tend 'grew'\n")
	out.append("\treturn c + a\n")
	out.append("end '{name}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'smallFunctionSource'

function smallFunctionName(fileIndex KnobSize, slot KnobSize) returns String
	return "sf{fileIndex}_{slot}"
end 'smallFunctionName'

// Each knob's ENTRY POINT is named once, in a constant like this one, and read by the two parties that
// must agree: the generator below, which emits the function under this name, and `generateRung`, which
// registers it as the knob's `RungDriver` so `main` calls it. They cannot drift, because there is
// nothing to drift from. (The chain knob is the exception, and it says why at `chainTopName`.)
let LongIfDriver = "scaleLongIf"

// The `longFunction` knob: ONE function, N sequential `if`s, over a bounded working set and
// `localCount` scope-fillers. At rung 5 this is the 3,200-`if` benchmark ARCHITECTURE.md quotes a
// measurement for. It grows OPS in a near-straight line; the CFG grows with it but stays a simple
// chain.
//
// Three things here are load-bearing, and the knob measured nothing without the first two (see
// `LocalsPerFunctionBase`):
//
//   • `localCount` SCOPE-FILLERS, so V climbs and the parser's per-branch O(vars-in-scope) snapshot
//     has something to climb WITH. Every filler is in scope at every `if` below it, and there are V of
//     them at the top rung. It used to be a fixed handful.
//   • Each branch mutates a ROTATING SUBSET of the bounded WORKING set, never the whole thing. A branch
//     that wrote every var in scope would make "cost O(vars in scope)" and "cost O(vars assigned)" the
//     same number, and the corpus could not tell the two apart — precisely the distinction that
//     decides whether a write-trail is worth building. Here in-scope is V+6 and assigned is ≤3.
//   • The guard is a SHORT-CIRCUIT `and`, which is what real code writes and which — measured
//     2026-07-14, while fixing the `const` flags bug — appeared in this corpus exactly nowhere. A
//     codegen change to the commonest conditional shape in the language was invisible to the instrument.
function longFunctionSource(ifCount KnobSize, localCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the long-function knob — {ifCount} sequential ifs over {WorkingLocalsPerFunction} working locals and {localCount} scope-fillers.\n")
	out.append("function {LongIfDriver}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append(scopedLocalsDecl(localCount))
	for i in 0 upto ifCount 'eachIf'
		let p = i mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		let q = (i + 1) mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		let r = (i + 2) mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		out.append("\tif w{p} > {i} and w{q} < w{r} 'c{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tw{p} = w{p} + {i mod 5 + 1}\n")
		out.append("\t\tw{q} = w{q} + w{r}\n")
		out.append("\tend 'c{i}'\n")
	end 'eachIf'
	out.append(workingLocalsSumAndReturn())
	out.append("end '{LongIfDriver}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'longFunctionSource'

let DeepBlocksDriver = "scaleDeepBlocks"

// The `deepBlocks` knob: ONE function whose BLOCK count is huge. Each if/else is three blocks (then,
// else, continuation), so this lands near 3x its knob — ~19,200 blocks at rung 5, which is the
// shape that used to SIGSEGV and the reason "no CFG traversal may recurse" is a rule rather than a
// preference. Distinct from `longFunction`: that grows ops along a chain, this grows the CFG. It
// carries the same `localCount` scope-fillers, so the V dimension is measured against a DIAMOND CFG
// here and a straight chain there.
// ⚠ THE TWO ARMS MUTATE DIFFERENT WORKING LOCALS, DELIBERATELY. That is what makes the merge do real
// work: each arm leaves a different subset changed, so the join genuinely has to reconcile them. When
// both arms wrote the SAME single `acc`, every merge was the trivial one-phi case and the
// snapshot-and-reconcile cost this knob is supposed to provoke never appeared.
function deepBlocksSource(ifElseCount KnobSize, localCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the deep-blocks knob — {ifElseCount} if/elses, ~{ifElseCount * 3} blocks, {WorkingLocalsPerFunction} working locals and {localCount} scope-fillers.\n")
	out.append("function {DeepBlocksDriver}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append(scopedLocalsDecl(localCount))
	for i in 0 upto ifElseCount 'eachIfElse'
		let p = i mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		let q = (i + 1) mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		out.append("\tif w{p} > {i} 'd{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tw{p} = w{p} + {i mod 3 + 1}\n")
		out.append("\tend 'd{i}' else 'e{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tw{q} = w{q} - {i mod 2 + 1}\n")
		out.append("\tend 'e{i}'\n")
	end 'eachIfElse'
	out.append(workingLocalsSumAndReturn())
	out.append("end '{DeepBlocksDriver}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'deepBlocksSource'

// One link of the chain, by index — the `smallFunctionName` pattern: ONE name function, read by the
// generator that emits the links and by the driver lookup below. The chain takes a name FUNCTION
// where every other knob takes a name constant, because it generates N functions rather than one.
function chainLinkName(link KnobSize) returns String
	return "scaleChain{link}"
end 'chainLinkName'

// The chain's DRIVER: its TOP link, the only one `main` calls (it reaches the rest by recursion).
//
// This is the awkward knob — its entry point's name is a function of the knob's SIZE, so it cannot be
// a constant like every other driver. It is a named function rather than a `chainLinkName(length - 1)`
// spelled at the call site because "the top link is `length - 1`" is a fact about the GENERATOR's
// numbering, and it belongs beside the loop that decides it: widen that loop and this moves with it,
// where a `- 1` sitting in `generateRung` would quietly go on naming a link in the middle.
function chainTopName(length KnobSize) returns String
	return chainLinkName(length - 1)
end 'chainTopName'

// The `callChain` knob: N functions, each calling the one below it and holding values LIVE ACROSS
// that call. The live values are the point — a bare call chain would measure call lowering, but
// values live across a call can only live in the 5 callee-saved registers, so this is what
// exercises the cross-call spill bracket.
//
// ⚠ IT NUMBERS THE LINKS 0..length-1, WHICH IS THE FACT `chainTopName` READS. Change the numbering
// here and change it there; nothing checks the pair, and a stale `chainTopName` names a MIDDLE link
// silently (`main` would still call a real function, and the knob would quietly shorten).
function callChainSource(length KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the call-chain knob — {length} chained calls, values live across each.\n")
	// The base of the chain: takes no call, so the recursion of generated text terminates.
	out.append("function {chainLinkName(0)}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn a + 1\n")
	out.append("end '{chainLinkName(0)}'\n")

	for i in 1 upto length 'eachLink'
		out.append("\nfunction {chainLinkName(i)}(a int) returns int\n")
		out.append("\tlet x = a + {i mod 9 + 1}\n")
		out.append("\tlet y = a * 2\n")
		out.append("\tlet z = {chainLinkName(i - 1)}(a)\n")
		out.append("\treturn x + y + z\n")
		out.append("end '{chainLinkName(i)}'\n")
	end 'eachLink'
	return out.build()
end 'callChainSource'

let PressuredDriver = "scalePressured"

// The `pressuredLoops` knob: ONE function, N loops, each holding PressuredValuesPerLoop
// accumulators live across a call to `scaleOpaque`. The splitter must spill the accumulators that do
// not fit the five callee-saved registers, so this is the knob that exercises the splitter hardest.
//
// ⚠ IT IS LINEAR NOW — see `PressuredLoopsBase`, which carries the full history. It USED to be the
// superlinear knob, because the splitter's driver recomputed liveness from scratch after every split
// (O(function × splits)); `49b160ce2` made that driver incremental and `regalloc:splitting` /
// `regalloc:liveness` both read ~x2.0 since. Do not restore a "KNOWN SUPERLINEAR" claim here without
// measuring it: that claim outlived its bug twice already (once here, once at the parser).
//
// It does NOT trip E5001 — see PressuredValuesPerLoop.
function pressuredLoopsSource(loopCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the pressured-loops knob — {loopCount} loops, {PressuredValuesPerLoop} values live across a call in each.\n")
	out.append("function {PressuredDriver}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar total = a\n")

	for loopIndex in 0 upto loopCount 'eachLoop'
		// Fresh accumulator names per loop, so the loops' live ranges do not overlap and peak
		// pressure stays at one loop's worth however many loops there are.
		for v in 0 upto PressuredValuesPerLoop 'declValues'
			out.append("\tvar s{loopIndex}_{v} = a + {v + 1}\n")
		end 'declValues'
		out.append("\tvar i{loopIndex} = 0\n")
		out.append("\twhile i{loopIndex} < {GeneratedLoopTrips} 'p{loopIndex}'\n")
		// The call is what makes the loop pressured: every accumulator is live across it, and a
		// value live across a call is forbidden all 9 caller-saved registers.
		out.append("\t\tlet r{loopIndex} = {OpaqueCalleeName}(i{loopIndex})\n")
		for v in 0 upto PressuredValuesPerLoop 'updateValues'
			out.append("\t\ts{loopIndex}_{v} = s{loopIndex}_{v} + r{loopIndex}\n")
		end 'updateValues'
		out.append("\t\ti{loopIndex} = i{loopIndex} + 1\n")
		out.append("\tend 'p{loopIndex}'\n")

		for v in 0 upto PressuredValuesPerLoop 'sumValues'
			out.append("\ttotal = total + s{loopIndex}_{v}\n")
		end 'sumValues'
	end 'eachLoop'

	out.append("\treturn total\n")
	out.append("end '{PressuredDriver}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'pressuredLoopsSource'

// The condition every nested loop tests. Large enough that the loop is not trivially one iteration —
// the compiler never RUNS these programs, so the exact bound is irrelevant to the measurement; only
// the loop's SHAPE is. It is compared against the opaque `acc`, so the folder cannot see through it.
let NestedLoopLimit = 1000000

let NestedDriver = "scaleNested"

// The `loopNest` knob: ONE function, ONE chain of loops NESTED to `depth`. Distinct from
// `pressuredLoops` (loop COUNT at depth 1): this grows loop DEPTH, which is what the loop-phi token
// pre-scan climbs — `parseWhileStatement` re-scans a loop's whole token extent, and a loop nested
// inside another is re-scanned once per enclosing loop, so this drives that scan O(depth x tokens).
//
// EVERY loop tests and mutates the SINGLE carried accumulator `acc`. That is deliberate: it keeps peak
// register pressure at one value however deep the nest goes (a per-level counter would be live across
// every enclosing loop and trip E5001 at depth ~14 — see `LoopNestBase`), and it keeps the loops
// fold-resistant, because `acc` originates in the opaque seed.
//
// ⚠ EXPECT THIS TO READ ~LINEAR IN MEMORY EVEN IF THE SCAN IS QUADRATIC IN TIME. The scan allocates
// ~one `AssignedNameSet` per loop (linear in depth) and each loop mints one phi for `acc` (linear in
// depth); its O(depth x tokens) cost is spent WALKING tokens, which allocates nothing. scale-test
// measures memory only, so a time-only superlinearity is invisible here BY DESIGN — this knob is the
// manifest's worked example of that blind spot, not a curve expected to bend.
function nestedLoopsSource(depth KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the loop-nest knob — one chain of {depth} loops nested to depth {depth}, all carrying one accumulator.\n")
	out.append("function {NestedDriver}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = a\n")

	// One pass: append each loop's opener, and PREPEND its `end` to a separate string. Prepending is
	// what puts the closers in innermost-first order (`n{depth-1}` … `n0`) with no reverse index — a
	// backward count would have to reach 0, which a ranged loop counter cannot represent.
	var closes = ""
	for d in 0 upto depth 'open'
		out.append("{tabs(d + 1)}while acc < {NestedLoopLimit} 'n{d}'\n")
		out.append("{tabs(d + 2)}acc = acc + {d + 1}\n")
		closes = "{tabs(d + 1)}end 'n{d}'\n{closes}"
	end 'open'

	out.append(closes)
	out.append("\treturn acc\n")
	out.append("end '{NestedDriver}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'nestedLoopsSource'

// The float knob's OPAQUE ORIGIN, and the conversion band's first direction.
//
// `a + 0.5` promotes the opaque int PARAMETER into the float domain, so a float seed is
// fold-resistant for exactly the reason every other value in this corpus is — shv2 has no
// interprocedural constant propagation, so `a` is opaque at every call site. A pile of arithmetic on
// float LITERALS would fold to nothing and measure an empty compile, which is this file's founding
// trap.
//
// It is also the int→float promotion itself (`5 + 2.0` is 7.0 — specs-shv2/float-type.md's
// `float-promotion`), so the way into the float domain is exercised by the same line that defeats
// the folder. `trunc` is the way out, and `floatSumAndReturn` is where every float function takes it.
function floatWorkingDecl() returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	for f in 0 upto WorkingFloatsPerFunction 'decl'
		out.append("\tvar f{f} = a + {f}.5\n")
	end 'decl'
	return out.build()
end 'floatWorkingDecl'

// The shared float epilogue: sum the accumulators and LEAVE the float domain through `trunc`.
//
// `trunc` is not a flourish, it is the only exit: a float RETURN needs the xmm0 return slot Wave 1
// does not model (specs-shv2/float-type.md's disabled `float-return-from-function`), so an `int`
// return through `fpToSi` is how a float value reaches `main` at all — and it is the conversion
// band's second direction, the one `floatWorkingDecl` does not cover.
function floatSumAndReturn() returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\tvar fsum = 0.0\n")
	for f in 0 upto WorkingFloatsPerFunction 'sum'
		out.append("\tfsum = fsum + f{f}\n")
	end 'sum'
	out.append("\treturn trunc(fsum)\n")
	return out.build()
end 'floatSumAndReturn'

let FloatsDriver = "scaleFloats"

// The `floatOps` knob: ONE function, N groups of float arithmetic and float compares over a bounded
// set of float accumulators. The float twin of `longFunction` — it grows float ops along a chain.
//
// Each group covers, deliberately, the things a float codegen change can move:
//   • ALL FOUR arithmetic ops. `/` matters most: it is the ONE operator whose float form is a
//     different Std op from its integer form (integer `/` traps and lowers to `StdOp.div`; float `/`
//     is `binOp(div)`), so a corpus without it measures three quarters of the arithmetic band.
//   • A float compare in BRANCH form, feeding a `jcc` — and the FLOAT PHI its merge takes, because
//     the accumulator is assigned on one arm only.
//   • The same compare MATERIALIZED as a bool, which is a different lowering: it lands `ucomisd`'s
//     answer in a GPR instead of feeding a jump, and it is the shape that crosses register FILES.
function floatOpsSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the float-ops knob — {groupCount} float arith/compare groups over {WorkingFloatsPerFunction} float accumulators.\n")
	out.append("function {FloatsDriver}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append(floatWorkingDecl())

	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		let p = i mod WorkingFloatsPerFunction
		let q = (i + 1) mod WorkingFloatsPerFunction
		let r = (i + 2) mod WorkingFloatsPerFunction

		out.append("\tf{p} = f{p} + f{q}\n")
		out.append("\tf{q} = f{q} - f{r}\n")
		out.append("\tf{p} = f{p} * {i mod 3 + 2}.5\n")
		out.append("\tf{r} = f{r} / {i mod 4 + 2}.25\n")

		// The compare that feeds a branch, and the float phi the continuation takes for `f{p}`.
		out.append("\tif f{p} > f{q} 'fc{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tf{p} = f{p} + 1.5\n")
		out.append("\tend 'fc{i}'\n")

		// The compare whose answer becomes a bool VALUE in a GPR before anything branches on it.
		out.append("\tlet fb{i} = f{r} < f{q}\n")
		out.append("\tif fb{i} 'fm{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tf{r} = f{r} + 0.25\n")
		out.append("\tend 'fm{i}'\n")
	end 'eachGroup'

	out.append(floatSumAndReturn())
	out.append("end '{FloatsDriver}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'floatOpsSource'

let FloatSpillDriver = "scaleFloatSpill"

// The `floatSpill` knob: ONE function, N loops, each holding `FloatsLivePerSpillLoop` floats live
// across a call to `scaleOpaque`. Every one of them is a FORCED spill — see `FloatSpillLoopsBase`
// for why the allowed set is ∅ rather than merely small.
//
// The call is an INTEGER function, and that is not a compromise: a float ARGUMENT and a float RETURN
// are both Wave 2, so an int callee is the only call a float can currently be live across — and it
// is precisely the shape that forces the spill, because the floats are live across the bracket and
// have nowhere caller-saved to survive it.
//
// Its accumulators are assigned INSIDE the loop, so each is a loop-carried FLOAT PHI. Before the
// `mintPhi` fix this knob's every rung would have panicked the x64 emitter outright — which is the
// most direct evidence available that the ladder needed this dimension.
//
// ⚠ WHY THIS IS NOT `pressuredLoopsSource` WITH A PREFIX PARAMETER, though the two emit the same
// SHAPE (declare, loop, call, update, sum). They are the same shape asked of two register files that
// answer it DIFFERENTLY, and the difference is the entire point of having both:
//
//   • `pressuredLoops` measures a CHOICE. Its eight GPRs have five callee-saved registers to compete
//     for, so the allocator decides which three go to memory, and the knob is tuned (eight, against a
//     14-GPR pool) to make exactly five spills fall out of that competition.
//   • `floatSpill` measures NO CHOICE. Its allowed set is ∅, so every float spills at every call at
//     any pool size and any knob value — there is nothing to tune and nothing to compete for.
//
// Merging them would take a `varPrefix`, a `counterPrefix`, a `resultPrefix`, a per-loop count, a
// literal suffix, an accumulator initializer and a return expression — seven parameters to fold two
// twenty-line text emitters, and the merged body would state neither reason above. Worse, the merge
// would couple them: retuning the GPR knob to keep provoking five spills would silently retune a
// float knob for which the number is irrelevant. Two knobs that a future change must be free to move
// independently are not one knob. (The file already works this way — `longFunction` and `deepBlocks`
// share a shape and stay apart for the same kind of reason.)
function floatSpillSource(loopCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the float-spill knob — {loopCount} loops, {FloatsLivePerSpillLoop} floats live across a call in each (allowed = ∅, so every one is a forced spill).\n")
	out.append("function {FloatSpillDriver}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar total = 0.0\n")

	for loopIndex in 0 upto loopCount 'eachLoop'
		// Fresh names per loop, so the loops' live ranges do not overlap and peak XMM pressure stays
		// at one loop's worth however many loops there are — `pressuredLoops`' rule, in the other file.
		for v in 0 upto FloatsLivePerSpillLoop 'declValues'
			out.append("\tvar g{loopIndex}_{v} = a + {v + 1}.25\n")
		end 'declValues'
		out.append("\tvar j{loopIndex} = 0\n")
		out.append("\twhile j{loopIndex} < {GeneratedLoopTrips} 'q{loopIndex}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tlet k{loopIndex} = {OpaqueCalleeName}(j{loopIndex})\n")

		for v in 0 upto FloatsLivePerSpillLoop 'updateValues'
			out.append("\t\tg{loopIndex}_{v} = g{loopIndex}_{v} + (k{loopIndex} + 0.5)\n")
		end 'updateValues'

		out.append("\t\tj{loopIndex} = j{loopIndex} + 1\n")
		out.append("\tend 'q{loopIndex}'\n")

		for v in 0 upto FloatsLivePerSpillLoop 'sumValues'
			out.append("\ttotal = total + g{loopIndex}_{v}\n")
		end 'sumValues'
	end 'eachLoop'

	out.append("\treturn trunc(total)\n")
	out.append("end '{FloatSpillDriver}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'floatSpillSource'

// The name prefixes the five knobs below declare and then read back. Each is spelled in a DECLARATION
// and in a USE many lines apart, so it is named once here for the reason `smallFunctionName` exists: a
// prefix written down twice is a prefix that can drift, and the failure is not a bad measurement but a
// generated program that does not compile at all.
let GlobalConstPrefix = "gc"
let GlobalVarPrefix = "gv"
let StructTypePrefix = "ScaleType"
let AllocTypePrefix = "ScaleAlloc"

let GlobalConstsDriver = "scaleGlobalConsts"

// The `globalConsts` knob: N top-level `let`s, each derived from the TWO below it, and a function
// that reads every one of them.
//
// ⚠ THIS KNOB EMITS ALMOST NO CODE FOR ITS SIZE, AND THAT IS THE FOLDER DOING ITS JOB RATHER THAN
// BEATING THE CORPUS. This file's founding trap — a benchmark that folds away — does NOT apply here,
// and a reader who "fixes" these to be fold-resistant will have made them not-constants and measured
// something else. A top-level `let` IS a constant: `let A = 5` then `acc + A` folds to `acc + 5`, and
// the fold is CORRECT. What this knob measures is the FRONT END — the constant arena, the initializer
// DFS, the memo key — all of which are reached at DECLARATION, before codegen has an opinion. The
// other knobs carry `assertCorpusCompiled`'s code-growth floor; this one is not asked to.
//
// It is still USED, on purpose: an unread constant is one a future pass may drop before the arena is
// ever built, which would take the dimension away again exactly as an uncalled function would (see
// `mainSource`). Reading it is what makes the declaration load-bearing.
//
// EACH CONSTANT REFERENCES THE TWO BELOW IT, and that is the property here worth defending. Over a
// CHAIN a DFS is O(N) whether it memoizes or not, so a chain cannot tell the two apart. Over a
// 2-PARENT DAG the memoized DFS is O(N) and the unmemoized one is O(2^N) — so if a change ever drops
// the memo, this knob does not bend politely, it HANGS at the first rung where 2^N stops being
// payable. That is the loudest failure this file can arrange for a cost that is otherwise invisible:
// the DFS allocates almost nothing, and a memory instrument would never see it bend.
function globalConstsSource(constCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the global-consts knob — {constCount} top-level `let`s, each derived from the two below it.\n")

	// The two seeds are literals; every constant above them derives from its two predecessors, which
	// is what makes the initializer graph a DAG rather than a chain.
	out.append("let {GlobalConstPrefix}0 = 1\n")
	out.append("let {GlobalConstPrefix}1 = 2\n")
	for c in 2 upto constCount 'eachConst'
		out.append("let {GlobalConstPrefix}{c} = ({GlobalConstPrefix}{c - 1} + {GlobalConstPrefix}{c - 2}) mod {GlobalConstModulus}\n")
	end 'eachConst'

	out.append("\n")
	out.append("function {GlobalConstsDriver}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = a\n")
	for c in 0 upto constCount 'eachUse'
		out.append("\tacc = acc + {GlobalConstPrefix}{c}\n")
	end 'eachUse'
	out.append("\treturn acc\n")
	out.append("end '{GlobalConstsDriver}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'globalConstsSource'

let GlobalVarsDriver = "scaleGlobalVars"

// The `globalVars` knob: N top-level `var`s, each stored to and loaded back through the opaque seed.
//
// ⚠ A GLOBAL IS FOLD-RESISTANT FOR FREE, AND IT HAS TO BE, BECAUSE IT CANNOT BE SEEDED. A top-level
// `var`'s initializer must be a CONSTANT expression — `var b = scaleSeed()` is E2045, and even
// `var b = a + 1` over another global is E2004 (`Undefined constant 'a'`) — so the two tricks the rest
// of this corpus relies on (a loop-carried var, a call result) are both unavailable at the point of
// declaration. They are also unnecessary: `StdOp.loadIndirect` is `isPure: false`, so a global READ is
// opaque to the folder no matter what the global was initialized to. The store below writes the opaque
// `acc`, and the load reads a location the folder must assume anything could have written.
//
// That impurity is a PROPERTY, not an accident, and it is pinned by specs-shv2/global-load-not-hoisted.md
// — a pure `loadIndirect` gets hoisted out of a loop that writes it, and the program then reads a stale
// value for ever. It is a silent wrong answer, so this knob must never be "fixed" by making the load
// pure to tidy up a number.
//
// Only `acc` and one loaded value are ever live, so the knob measures `.data` layout and the
// address/load/store triple, and can never quietly become a register-pressure knob and trip E5001.
function globalVarsSource(varCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the global-vars knob — {varCount} top-level `var`s in .data, each stored to and loaded back.\n")
	for v in 0 upto varCount 'eachVar'
		out.append("var {GlobalVarPrefix}{v} = {v mod GlobalVarSeedModulus}\n")
	end 'eachVar'

	out.append("\n")
	out.append("function {GlobalVarsDriver}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = a\n")
	for v in 0 upto varCount 'eachUse'
		out.append("\t{GlobalVarPrefix}{v} = {GlobalVarPrefix}{v} + acc\n")
		out.append("\tacc = acc + {GlobalVarPrefix}{v}\n")
	end 'eachUse'
	out.append("\treturn acc\n")
	out.append("end '{GlobalVarsDriver}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'globalVarsSource'

// One `type` declaration: two fields, and the explicit `static create` that is the only way to build
// one today.
//
// ⚠ THE `static create` IS NOT OPTIONAL, AND ITS ABSENCE IS NOT A DIAGNOSTIC YOU WOULD ENJOY. A `type`
// with no explicit `static function create` does not resolve at the USE site, and the field read fails
// with `E2015: a field access on 'p', which is declared 'unknown'` — a diagnostic that points at the
// reader of the field rather than at the missing constructor. (Measured, while writing this knob.)
//
// The fields are plain `int` rather than a ranged typealias. specs-shv2/structs.md reaches for an
// alias, but every generated function in this corpus already takes `a int`, an `int` field lays out
// the same 8-byte slot the alias would, and going without drops a file-private typealias from each
// struct file for no loss of coverage — ranged typealiases are `shiftCoverageSource`'s job.
//
// SHARED BY BOTH STRUCT KNOBS, so the two measure the SAME declaration against different dimensions:
// how many of them exist, and how many times one is built.
function structTypeDecl(name String) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\ntype {name}\n")
	out.append("\texport var x as int\n")
	out.append("\texport var y as int\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("\tstatic function create(x int, y int) returns Self\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn Self\{x: x, y: y\}\n")
	out.append("\tend 'create'\n")
	out.append("end '{name}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'structTypeDecl'

// ⭐ EVERY `create` IN THIS FILE SITS IN ITS OWN BLOCK, AND THE REASON IS MEASURED, NOT STYLISTIC.
// Both struct knobs emit through here, so the rule is stated once.
//
// A STRUCT BINDING IS OWNED, AND AN OWNED BINDING DOES NOT DIE WHEN YOU STOP READING IT. At
// function-body scope it stays on the parser's `ownedBindings` list until the `return`, where
// `emitScopeDrops` (Parser.maxon:2524) decrefs every one of them. So N creates at function scope put
// N struct pointers LIVE SIMULTANEOUSLY at the return, against a 14-GPR pool — and the splitter must
// then spill nearly all of them. It does not refuse (they are spillable, so this is never E5001); it
// goes QUADRATIC.
//
// MEASURED, on a private ladder of 32/64/128/256/512 creates in one function:
//   • at function scope: `regalloc:splitting` reads x3.49, x3.70, x3.84, x3.91 per doubling —
//     converging on x4.00, and 65% of the whole compile at the top rung.
//   • in per-create blocks: x1.82, x1.90, x1.95, x1.97. LINEAR, and the knob measures what it says.
// The mechanism is `SplitLiveRanges`' driver: it is incremental in DIRTY BLOCKS, so one enormous
// block means every one of the ~N splits re-sweeps all O(N) of its ops.
//
// ⚠ IT DOES NOT DEPEND ON THE `closeBlock` LEAK, WHICH IS THE FIRST THING A READER WILL SUSPECT.
// `closeBlock` (Parser.maxon:2343) drains the binding and deliberately emits NO drop, so a struct in
// a block leaks today (exit 101, a stated P1.4 gap this instrument never observes because it COMPILES
// these programs and never RUNS them). That leak is not what bounds the live range, and the knob does
// not want it to be: when P1.4 emits the drop at the block's END, the live range is bounded by the
// block either way. Correct before the fix and after it — which is the only reason it is safe to lean
// on a construct with a known gap in it.
//
// The `if` is SCOPE SCAFFOLDING, not a CFG dimension — `deepBlocks` owns that one, and this adds ~1
// branch per create against its ~3 blocks per if/else. `pressuredLoops` uses `while` the same way, for
// the same reason: a knob may spend control flow to set up the thing it actually measures.
function structCreateInBlock(index KnobSize, typeName String, binding String) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\tif acc > 0 'g{index}'\n")
	out.append("\t\tlet {binding} = {typeName}.create(acc, y: acc + {index mod 5 + 1})\n")
	out.append("\t\tacc = acc + {binding}.x + {binding}.y\n")
	out.append("\tend 'g{index}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'structCreateInBlock'

let StructTypesDriver = "scaleStructTypes"

// The `structTypes` knob: N `type` declarations, each built exactly ONCE.
//
// The per-TYPE cost is the point — the type table and the layout descriptor — so what doubles is how
// many types EXIST, not how hard they are used. Each is instantiated once anyway, because a type
// nothing ever builds is a type a future pass may legitimately never lay out, and the dimension would
// evaporate silently. That is `mainSource`'s rule one level down: an unbuilt type is an uncalled
// function's twin.
//
// ⚠ THERE IS NO `__destruct_*` CASCADE TO MEASURE YET, whatever this knob's name suggests. The heap's
// runtime is five FIXED functions (`installMmRuntime`), and a per-type destructor is a later rung —
// `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:9` names it as future work ("an `own.drop` into a `__destruct_T` call"). When
// it lands it will be a per-TYPE cost, and this is the knob it will show up in.
function structTypesSource(typeCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the struct-types knob — {typeCount} type declarations, each built once.\n")
	for t in 0 upto typeCount 'eachType'
		out.append(structTypeDecl("{StructTypePrefix}{t}"))
	end 'eachType'

	out.append("\nfunction {StructTypesDriver}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = a\n")
	for t in 0 upto typeCount 'eachUse'
		out.append(structCreateInBlock(t, typeName: "{StructTypePrefix}{t}", binding: "s{t}"))
	end 'eachUse'
	out.append("\treturn acc\n")
	out.append("end '{StructTypesDriver}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'structTypesSource'

let StructAllocsDriver = "scaleStructAllocs"

// The `structAllocs` knob: M `create` sites over a FIXED `AllocStructTypeCount` types.
//
// The per-SITE cost is the point — an `__mm_alloc` call, a field store per field, and the release the
// value's death forces — so what doubles is the number of ALLOCATION SITES while the number of types
// stays put. That is the whole of the difference from `structTypes`, and the reason the two are not one
// knob with a bigger number: this is the column that moves when P1.1a wave 2 changes what a `create`
// emits, and that one is the column that moves when the layout descriptor changes.
//
// Every create is block-scoped for the reason `structCreateInBlock` states — measured at x3.96 per
// doubling without it, x1.97 with it.
function structAllocsSource(allocCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the struct-allocs knob — {allocCount} create sites over {AllocStructTypeCount} types.\n")
	for t in 0 upto AllocStructTypeCount 'eachType'
		out.append(structTypeDecl("{AllocTypePrefix}{t}"))
	end 'eachType'

	out.append("\nfunction {StructAllocsDriver}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = a\n")
	for i in 0 upto allocCount 'eachAlloc'
		out.append(structCreateInBlock(i, typeName: "{AllocTypePrefix}{i mod AllocStructTypeCount}", binding: "p{i}"))
	end 'eachAlloc'
	out.append("\treturn acc\n")
	out.append("end '{StructAllocsDriver}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'structAllocsSource'

let DivideDriver = "scaleDivide"

// The `intDivide` knob: N `/` and `mod` groups over a divisor derived from the opaque seed.
//
// BOTH OPERATORS, because they share one `idiv` and read DIFFERENT result registers — the quotient
// from RAX, the remainder from RDX. A corpus with only one of them measures half the lowering.
//
// The dividend is `acc`, which the group has just written and the next group will read, so `acc` is
// live ACROSS an `idiv` that clobbers both RAX and RDX. That is the constraint the whole path exists
// to satisfy, and a corpus of `20 / 4` could never provoke it — that folds before it reaches an
// allocator at all.
function intDivideSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the int-divide knob — {groupCount} `/` and `mod` groups over an opaque, non-zero divisor.\n")
	out.append("function {DivideDriver}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = a\n")
	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		out.append("\tlet d{i} = (acc and {DivisorMask}) + 1\n")
		out.append("\tacc = acc + acc / d{i}\n")
		out.append("\tacc = acc + acc mod d{i}\n")
	end 'eachGroup'
	out.append("\treturn acc\n")
	out.append("end '{DivideDriver}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'intDivideSource'

// The mask that bounds a dynamic shift COUNT to a sane, non-negative range. A count derived this way
// is a runtime value the folder cannot see, so it takes the GUARDED shift path — the 6-7 ops that
// saturate an out-of-range count — which a constant count folds away and which nothing in this corpus
// exercised before. This is also the corpus's bitwise `and` on plain ints (the guards' `and` is the
// short-circuit one, over bools).
let ShiftCountMask = 63

let ShiftsDriver = "scaleShifts"

// The shift COVERAGE shapes. These do NOT scale — they are about COVERAGE, not the ladder: their
// value is that a future codegen change to shifts is no longer invisible, the way the `const`-flags
// fix was invisible because the corpus had no `and`/`or`. A shift codegen change moves these functions'
// emitted bytes, and the trend delta reports ANY movement, constant or not.
//
// Four axes are covered that the corpus had at ZERO before:
//   • CONSTANT count (folded at compile time) vs DYNAMIC count (a runtime value, guarded).
//   • SIGNED operand (arithmetic / sign fill) vs RANGED `int(0 to u64.max)` operand (logical / zero
//     fill). The fill is decided by the LEFT operand's type, and this is the shape that once hid a
//     fold-vs-codegen disagreement (const=0 vs dyn=-4294967296).
function shiftCoverageSource() returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: shift COVERAGE (fixed, not scaled) — const/dynamic counts over signed and ranged operands.\n")
	out.append("typealias ScaleWide = int(0 to u64.max)\n")
	out.append("typealias ScaleSigned = int(i64.min to i64.max)\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// Dynamic count, signed operand: the guarded shl then shr.
	out.append("function scaleShiftDyn(v ScaleSigned, c ScaleSigned) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tlet x = v shl c\n")
	out.append("\treturn x shr c\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleShiftDyn'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// Constant count, ranged (unsigned) operand: zero-fill decided by the LEFT operand's range.
	out.append("function scaleShiftWideConst(v ScaleWide) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn v shr 60\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleShiftWideConst'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// Dynamic count, ranged operand: guarded, and the fill still decided by the operand's range.
	out.append("function scaleShiftWideDyn(v ScaleWide, c ScaleSigned) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn v shr c\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleShiftWideDyn'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// Constant count, signed operand: arithmetic (sign) fill.
	out.append("function scaleShiftSignedConst(v ScaleSigned) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn v shr 60\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleShiftSignedConst'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// The driver threads the opaque seed through every shift function. The seed `a` flows straight into
	// every operand, signed OR ranged: shv2's argument check is CLASS-based (numeric agrees with
	// numeric), so a plain `int` coerces into a `ScaleWide` parameter with no cast and no mask. The
	// operand's VALUE never matters here — the shift codegen inside each callee is exercised because the
	// parameter is opaque (shv2 has no interprocedural constant propagation), not because of what flows
	// in. Dynamic counts are the seed masked to a small non-negative range so they stay runtime values.
	out.append("function {ShiftsDriver}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = a\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc + scaleShiftWideConst(a)\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc + scaleShiftWideDyn(a, c: acc and {ShiftCountMask})\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc + scaleShiftSignedConst(acc)\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc + scaleShiftDyn(acc, c: acc and {ShiftCountMask})\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc + (acc shl 3)\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc + (acc shr 2)\n")
	out.append("\treturn acc\n")
	out.append("end '{ShiftsDriver}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'shiftCoverageSource'

// `main`, which threads the seed through every generated function.
//
// It calls all of them ON PURPOSE. An uncalled function is a function a future dead-code pass could
// legitimately delete, and the day that pass lands, the `manyFunctions` knob would silently
// evaporate and the ladder would go flat — reporting perfect linearity because it had stopped
// compiling anything. Threading `acc` through every call makes each one load-bearing.
//
// ⭐ EVERY name comes from `drivers`, the exact list the generators registered as they wrote each
// file — so NO enumeration is written down a second time here and none can drift from the names on
// disk. It used to be true of the `manyFunctions` loop alone, and this comment said so while the
// thirteen lines directly beneath it re-spelled every other knob's driver by hand: a comment
// contradicted by the code it sat on. Those thirteen are now `RungDriver.threads(...)` arguments at
// the site that writes each file, which is a place the compiler makes you fill in.
function mainSource(drivers StringArray) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: threads the opaque seed through every generated function.\n")
	out.append("function main() returns ExitCode\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = {SeedName}()\n")

	for name in drivers 'eachDriver'
		out.append("\tacc = {name}(acc)\n")
	end 'eachDriver'

	out.append("\tif acc > 0 'ok'\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn 0\n")
	out.append("\tend 'ok'\n")
	out.append("\treturn 1\n")
	out.append("end 'main'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'mainSource'

// ===========================================================================================
// The corpus self-check — THE reason anything the instrument reports can be believed
// ===========================================================================================
//
// The trap in this file's header is not hypothetical, and this is where it is caught. A corpus that
// folds away leaves the instrument measuring an empty compile at every rung; it would fit a perfect
// straight line through six near-zeroes and report flawless linearity. That is the most convincing
// possible wrong answer, and nothing downstream could catch it — every number would look lovely.
//
// So before anything is reported: did the compiler actually EMIT anything, and did it emit
// proportionally more of it as the program grew? Emitted code bytes are fitted against source bytes
// exactly as a phase's cost is, and a flat or sublinear result means the RUN IS BROKEN.
//
// This is the one condition the instrument exits NON-ZERO for, and the distinction is the whole
// design: a surprising number is a reading to explain, but a degenerate corpus did not produce a
// surprising reading — it produced no reading at all, about a compiler it never ran on a program
// that was never there.

// The floor each rung's emitted code must clear against the rung below it.
//
// THE LADDER DOUBLES, so a corpus that is really being compiled emits about TWICE the code at each
// rung — measured, x1.99 to x2.00 at every step. A corpus that folded away emits about the SAME code
// at every rung (x1.00), because what it emits is the fixed floor and nothing else. x1.50 sits in the
// wide gap between those two and is nowhere near either.
//
// It is deliberately not x2.00: real code size grows slightly SUBLINEARLY, because a rung's fixed
// overhead (the entry stub, the runtime floor) is amortized over more program as the ladder climbs.
export let MinCodeGrowthRatio = 150 as GrowthRatio

// A growth check needs two rungs. `--rungs=1` is a legal ladder — it measures one program, and its
// per-rung numbers are as exact as any other run's — but nothing about GROWTH can be said from it,
// this check included. The runner says so; it does not fail the run for a ladder it was asked for.
export let MinRungsForGrowth = 2

// EVERY rung is checked against the one below it, not just the ends of the ladder.
//
// That is strictly stronger than what it replaced (a power law fitted through all six code sizes,
// whose exponent had to clear a floor): a single rung that folded while its neighbours grew is a rung
// that stopped measuring the compiler, and a fit through the other five would absorb it and report a
// healthy exponent. A ratio per rung cannot absorb anything.
export function assertCorpusCompiled(results RungResultArray) throws ScaleError
	if results.count() < MinRungsForGrowth 'tooFewRungs'
		return
	end 'tooFewRungs'

	for i in 1 upto results.count() 'eachRung'
		let below = try results.get(i - 1) otherwise panic("assertCorpusCompiled: i >= 1 but results.get({i - 1}) failed")
		let rung = try results.get(i) otherwise panic("assertCorpusCompiled: i < count but results.get({i}) failed")

		if not grewBy(rung.codeBytes, previous: below.codeBytes, minimum: MinCodeGrowthRatio) 'folded'
			throw ScaleError.reason("rung {rung.rung} emitted {rung.codeBytes} bytes of code against rung {below.rung}'s {below.codeBytes} — a growth of {growthText(growthOf(rung.codeBytes, previous: below.codeBytes))} where the ladder DOUBLES the program and the floor is {growthText(GrowthReading.measured(MinCodeGrowthRatio))}. The corpus FOLDED AWAY rather than compiling, so every number this run produced is a number about nothing. The generated programs must be fold-resistant (see this file's header): seed every value from a loop-carried accumulator or a call result.")
		end 'folded'
	end 'eachRung'
end 'assertCorpusCompiled'

// The corpus's own growth, rung over rung — PRINTED, and in `--result-json`, because a reader looking
// at a set of memory numbers is entitled to see the evidence that they are numbers about a program
// that was actually compiled rather than one that folded away.
export function codeGrowthAt(results RungResultArray, rung LadderIndex) returns GrowthReading
	if rung == 0 'bottomRung'
		return GrowthReading.noBaseline
	end 'bottomRung'

	let below = try results.get(rung - 1) otherwise panic("codeGrowthAt: rung >= 1 but results.get({rung - 1}) failed")
	let here = try results.get(rung) otherwise panic("codeGrowthAt: results.get({rung}) failed")
	return growthOf(here.codeBytes, previous: below.codeBytes)
end 'codeGrowthAt'

// ===========================================================================================
// THE INSTRUMENT DECLARES WHAT IT CAN SEE — AND WHAT IT STRUCTURALLY CANNOT
// ===========================================================================================
//
// PRINTED ON EVERY RUN. An instrument that cannot state its own blind spots produces confident zeros:
// this corpus was blind in four measured dimensions at once — it grew no locals, nested no loops,
// shifted by no runtime value, and shifted no ranged operand — and every one of those blind spots was
// SILENT. Two independent reviews found five compiler defects the corpus could see NONE of. The
// through-line was always the same: the corpus finds only bugs someone already thought to generate. So
// the corpus now SAYS what it generates and, more importantly, what it does not and what it cannot —
// because the gaps are the part a reader cannot otherwise know, and a gap nobody is told about reads as
// coverage.
//
// ⚠ AND A MANIFEST CAN LIE BY OMISSION, WHICH IS WORSE THAN THE SILENCE IT REPLACED. This list read
// `let / var` under CONSTRUCTS GENERATED and meant the BODY-LOCAL kind — the only kind the corpus had.
// Top-level `let` and `var` were named under NEITHER heading, so two landed rungs and 36 spec tests
// read as covered by a reader doing exactly what this manifest asks of them. `structs` sat under NOT
// GENERATED for a rung after P1.0r implemented them, and integer `/` and `mod` sat there for three
// after P1.0d.3 built the entire idiv path.
//
// ⇒ THE RULE THIS LIST IS NOW HELD TO: every construct in the subset appears under EXACTLY ONE
// heading, and a construct that is PARTLY generated (structs — reads yes, writes no) says which part
// under GENERATED rather than being filed whole under either. A heading is a claim; an omission is a
// claim too, and it is the one nobody audits.
//
// TWO of these are STRUCTURAL — no knob can close them, so they are stated as blind spots rather than
// filed as TODOs:
//   • MEMORY ONLY. scale-test measures allocations and bytes, never time; a time-only superlinearity
//     is invisible here by construction, and the loop-nest knob's own scan is the worked example.
//   • THE SUBSET. shv2 compiles a fragment of Maxon, so whole language features generate nothing at
//     all, and a codegen change to any of them moves no number here.
export function printCorpusManifest()
	print("\n")
	print("scale-test: CORPUS MANIFEST — what this ladder exercises, and what it structurally cannot see.\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  CONSTRUCTS GENERATED (a codegen change to one of these moves the memory numbers):\n")
	print("    int arithmetic (+ - *), comparisons, BODY-LOCAL let / var, return\n")
	print("    integer / and mod — the idiv path, over an OPAQUE non-zero divisor, with the dividend\n")
	print("      live across the RAX/RDX clobber (a constant divisor folds and measures nothing)\n")
	print("    TOP-LEVEL let — the constant arena and its initializer DFS, as a 2-PARENT DAG so an\n")
	print("      unmemoized DFS would go exponential rather than merely slow. It is FOLDED into every\n")
	print("      use and emits almost no code: that is correct, and it is the front end being measured\n")
	print("    TOP-LEVEL var — .data slots, GlobalDataTable.layOut, globalAddr/loadIndirect/storeIndirect\n")
	print("    STRUCTS (the heap): `type` declarations, an explicit `static create`, Self\{\} construction,\n")
	print("      field READS, and the __mm_alloc site each create emits. TWO BLIND SPOTS LIVE INSIDE\n")
	print("      THIS ONE and no knob here can close them: field WRITES (`p.x = 30`) are P1.1a wave 2\n")
	print("      and NOT IMPLEMENTED, and a struct crossing a CALL boundary (as a parameter or a\n")
	print("      return) is P1.4, deferred on the borrow-vs-consume ruling. A codegen change to either\n")
	print("      is invisible here even though structs are otherwise generated\n")
	print("    if, if/else, while, and NESTED while (to a doubling depth)\n")
	print("    functions (<=6 params), cross-file merge, chained calls holding values live across them\n")
	print("    short-circuit `and` (the two-condition guards), bitwise `and` (shift-count masks)\n")
	print("    shl / shr with: a CONSTANT count (folded) AND a DYNAMIC count (runtime-guarded), over a\n")
	print("      SIGNED operand (arithmetic/sign fill) AND a ranged int(0 to u64.max) operand (zero fill)\n")
	print("    ranged typealiases\n")
	print("    FLOAT (the SSE band): + - * / , compares in BOTH forms (feeding a jcc, and materialized\n")
	print("      as a bool), float PHIs (branch-merged and loop-carried), int->float promotion and\n")
	print("      trunc (the conversion band's two directions), and floats live ACROSS A CALL — whose\n")
	print("      allowed set is EXACTLY empty, so every one is a forced spill\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  COST DIMENSIONS ON THE DOUBLING LADDER (each DOUBLES per rung; the rung-over-rung RATIO is\n")
	print("  the growth — x2 linear, x4 quadratic — read straight off the counts):\n")
	print("    function count, file count, sequential-if ops, block/CFG count, call-chain depth,\n")
	print("    pressured-loop COUNT, loop-nest DEPTH, locals-in-scope per function (the V dimension),\n")
	print("    float-op groups, float-spill loop COUNT, top-level `let` COUNT, top-level `var` COUNT,\n")
	print("    `type` declaration COUNT (a per-TYPE cost), struct `create` SITE count (a per-SITE cost,\n")
	print("    over a FIXED set of types, so the two move independently), and int-divide groups\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  NOT GENERATED — a codegen change to any of these is INVISIBLE here (structural blind spot):\n")
	print("    `break` (in the subset, but no generated loop exits early); `or` / `xor` / `not`;\n")
	print("    print, arrays, strings, `for`, `match`; closures, async, generics; `as` casts.\n")
	print("    Float ARGUMENTS and float RETURNS are absent because they do not exist yet (Wave 2 —\n")
	print("    the xmm0 ABI slot); every float here enters by promotion and leaves through trunc,\n")
	print("    which is the whole of what Wave 1 can express. Struct field WRITES and structs across a\n")
	print("    CALL are absent for the same reason — see the struct entry above, which names them\n")
	print("    where a reader looking up structs will actually find them.\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  !! MEASURES MEMORY ONLY — a TIME-only superlinearity is invisible here BY DESIGN. Time is not\n")
	print("     reproducible, so it cannot be trended; a cost that re-walks tokens or chases pointers but\n")
	print("     allocates little will read FLAT in these columns even when it is quadratic in time. The\n")
	print("     worked example is IN this corpus: the loop-nest knob drives the loop-phi token pre-scan\n")
	print("     (Parser.loopBodyEndIndex + namesAssignedIn) to O(depth x tokens), yet that scan allocates\n")
	print("     ~one small set per loop and reads roughly LINEAR here. For the time view of one compile,\n")
	print("     use `--log=compiler:debug` or `--metrics=<path>` — this instrument will not show it.\n")
	print("\n")
end 'printCorpusManifest'
